      "narrative": "Maricopa County’s residential market is cooling but not crashing, with median sale prices roughly flat to slightly higher than a year ago and showing modest month-over-month slippage in recent reports.[8][10][11][13][15] Inventory has moved from historically tight toward more balanced: active listings in the Phoenix-Mesa-Chandler metro are up year over year, days on market have stretched into roughly two to two-and-a-half months on average, and a growing share of homes see price cuts.[2][5][3][10][11][13] These conditions have shifted leverage toward patient buyers, as sales volumes and pending contracts are down compared with last year, while sellers who price aggressively or rely on early-pandemic bidding-war dynamics are seeing longer marketing times and reduced success rates.[7][10][13] Local drivers remain solid, major employers in tech, finance, healthcare and manufacturing continue to support the region, while elevated mortgage rates near the mid-6% range and the waning of post-pandemic in-migration are key forces tempering demand.[6][9][11]",
